          "id": "SP_BRIAREUM_CYLINDRUM_SAMIMI_NAMIN_VAN_OFWEGEN_2016",
          "name": "Briareum cylindrum Samimi-Namin & van Ofwegen, 2016",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Briareum",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "The holotype consists of several fragments of an encrusting colony, the largest being 4 by 1.5 cm in diameter (Figure 2 A) with white surface and magenta underside. Calyces hardly projecting. The calyces contain colourless, flattened rods with prominent simple tubercles (Figure 3 A, B). These rods are up to 0.20 mm long. The cortex contains colourless spindles, cylinders, and tripoids (Figure 3 C). All these forms have complex tubercles, often arranged in girdles. These sclerites can be up to 0.60 mm long but most are only 0.30 mm long. The medulla contains magenta spindles and branched spindles with simple or complex tubercles (Figure 4). These sclerites are 0.20 - 0.60 mm long. They can be fused into small clumps.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:7981088",
          "authorship": "Samimi-Namin & van Ofwegen",
          "yearPublished": "2016",
          "children": [],
          "uuid": "5d7cf0ad-b5e0-55f-aac-48c5f69ef68ccc",
          "sourceIds": {
            "col": [
              "692QM"
            ]
          },
          "authorityIds": "col:692QM"
        },
